pics, four other runners had broken four minutes for the mile – Hungary’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/71312"} "László Tábori"] ", two Britons, " [:a {:href "/athletes/68952"} "Chris Chataway"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/69138"} "Brian Hewson"] ", and Australian " [:a {:href "/athletes/64724"} "John Landy"] ". Landy hoped to win the 1,500 in front of his countrymen, and he had the best record of any of the contenders. Unlike 1952, there were only two rounds, which qualified 12 for the final, among them Landy, Tábori, Hewson, and Ireland’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/71519"} "Ron Delany"] ". The final was led out by Landy’s teammate, " [:a {:href "/athletes/64732"} "Merv Lincoln"] ". The field was closely bunched with Delany running at the rear. Hewson took the lead on the third lap and led thru 1,200 metres in 3:01.3. Delany was still near the rear but on the backstretch he moved up and passed Hewson at the head of the final straight, pulling away to win the gold medal in a major upset. Germany’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/69864"} "Klaus Richtzenhain"] " was second, while John Landy, who had run an indifferent race, finished strongly for the bronze medal."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "102"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/71519"} "Ronnie Delany"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/IRL"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/IRL.png"}] "IRL"]] [:td {} "3:47.4 (3 h2)"] [:td {} "3:41.2 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-default"} [:abbrev {:title "Olympic Record"} "OR"]]] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "134"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/69864"} "Klaus Richtzenhain"]] [:td {} [:a {